00:20:06,923 --> 00:20:08,766
Do we need to improve our resources?

207
00:20:09,647 --> 00:20:18,719
The other thing we wanted to know was when you see health promotion campaigns for mental health and substance use, does it translate into other languages?

208
00:20:18,759 --> 00:20:20,241
Does it culturally translate?

209
00:20:21,082 --> 00:20:23,304
Does it interpret well?

210
00:20:23,784 --> 00:20:24,905
So we did that work.

211
00:20:25,005 --> 00:20:33,151
We analyzed that research, and we've come out with some findings for that, which we have available if people want to read that and see that.

212
00:20:33,571 --> 00:20:45,800
And the next round of funding will be to bring groups of people together from different backgrounds, culturally and linguistic backgrounds, to develop campaigns that do work,

213
00:20:46,566 --> 00:20:49,507
in other languages that do work with other cultural backgrounds.

214
00:20:49,827 --> 00:20:51,848
So we'll be working on that over the next year.

215
00:20:51,868 --> 00:21:09,473
The other thing that we'll be doing is kicking up information to mental health providers, substance use providers, to hospitals and healthcare of what could be improvements that would really help the health of the many individuals in our community who are

216
00:21:11,590 --> 00:21:18,057
you know, who are culturally, just to be culturally and linguistically responsive, we have to do a better job in this community.

217
00:21:18,558 --> 00:21:20,219
So that's one of the grants.

218
00:21:20,239 --> 00:21:25,805
The other grant that we're working on is an evaluation of all the programs, most of the programs I just spoke to you about.

219
00:21:26,666 --> 00:21:32,913
We're working with Tufts University on that and they're really trying to figure out what is the data that we're collecting

220
00:21:33,993 --> 00:21:37,634
Will we be, you know, with all the programs that we have today, do they make sense?

221
00:21:37,694 --> 00:21:38,555
Are they streamlined?

222
00:21:38,575 --> 00:21:45,076
Do people know about what the health department is doing to outreach the community and to support our community?

223
00:21:45,436 --> 00:21:53,939
The theory that we're working from is if we can help families reduce the stigma of seeking care, get care,

224
00:21:54,910 --> 00:22:03,896
that we're going to help young people see how they can exercise that behavior as they go forward throughout their life, regardless if they do or they do not use substances.

225
00:22:03,916 --> 00:22:07,798
The idea is that we get help when we need help.

226
00:22:09,359 --> 00:22:10,480
So we're looking at that.

227
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:15,003
One of the big components of that grant, though, is looking at our Behavioral Health Commission.

228
00:22:15,883 --> 00:22:19,264
So we have the Promote, Prevent, Support Behavioral Health Commission.

229
00:22:19,304 --> 00:22:21,385
Mary Ann is the chair of that commission.

230
00:22:21,405 --> 00:22:43,271
I am assigned as the secretary of the commission, and it is a number of department heads that are forward-facing and do behavioral health work just by the way that the work comes at them, as well as appointed community members and some other folks from, you know, the hospital, from our EMS provider, Ameshire Ambulance,

231
00:22:44,518 --> 00:22:45,659
and Tufts University.

232
00:22:45,739 --> 00:22:46,999
So that's going really well.

233
00:22:47,019 --> 00:22:49,120
We have two subcommittees under that.

234
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:55,683
One is to look at what are the current plans, programs that we're offering to our community.

235
00:22:55,923 --> 00:23:09,709
We're going to map that out, figure out where the gaps are, look at it from multiple angles, you know, types of service as well as age, you know, target populations and see where gaps lie in the city for that.

236
00:23:10,209 --> 00:23:17,592
And then coupled with that is a very intensive data team that will look at how do we collect data that we already have?

237
00:23:17,832 --> 00:23:21,313
What data do we need to start collecting in the city to understand the issues?

238
00:23:21,614 --> 00:23:24,975
And then finally, how do we create a dashboard that we can all use?

239
00:23:25,455 --> 00:23:35,802
when we're applying for grant funding, when we're communicating to the community so that it's not, you know, a 30-step process to see what data looks like in the City of Medford.

240
00:23:36,222 --> 00:23:41,005
So that's one of the biggest projects, I think, that comes from that particular grant.

241
00:23:41,985 --> 00:23:44,227
And then finally, we have a regional grant that we host.

242
00:23:44,688 --> 00:23:48,671
We have been the host of a regional grant for the Mystic Valley Public Health Coalition.

243
00:23:49,672 --> 00:24:04,446
You know, even before, as you all know, if you've been around a long time, the emergency preparedness work, and then we did our opioid work through this coalition, and now we are leading a primary prevention delay first use of substances grant.

244
00:24:05,226 --> 00:24:06,227
It's an eight-year grant.

245
00:24:06,247 --> 00:24:09,068
We're completing our first year.

246
00:24:09,168 --> 00:24:14,931
We're working on an assessment across seven communities, Medford, Malden, Melrose, Stoneham, Wakefield, Redding, Winchester.

247
00:24:15,011 --> 00:24:16,072
Those are our communities.

248
00:24:17,293 --> 00:24:25,177
And once we have gotten our data collection, we're doing interviews, focus groups, gathering quantitative data, right?

249
00:24:26,017 --> 00:24:29,701
Once we do that, we're going to sit down and be doing a strategic plan across the region.

250
00:24:30,061 --> 00:24:36,608
That strategic plan, once approved by the state, we will start implementing it and we will be doing that work.

