262
00:25:20,369 --> 00:25:24,512
So we have a grant called the Municipal Vulnerability Preparedness Grant.

263
00:25:24,632 --> 00:25:27,014
We received a round of funding in January 2020.

264
00:25:27,454 --> 00:25:32,238
We did an assessment of, you know, if there was a climate event,

265
00:25:33,243 --> 00:25:35,607
What how would people seek information?

266
00:25:35,647 --> 00:25:36,929
How would they access information?

267
00:25:36,969 --> 00:25:37,790
How would they get help?

268
00:25:37,870 --> 00:25:38,130
Right.

269
00:25:38,251 --> 00:25:41,015
And so in that moment, we were in the middle of a pandemic.

270
00:25:41,255 --> 00:25:42,817
We got a lot of great information.

271
00:25:43,498 --> 00:25:48,025
And from that, we wrote a really well organized grant that looked at three different parts.

272
00:25:48,625 --> 00:25:54,527
One is how interdepartmentally are we communicating to support our community?

273
00:25:54,787 --> 00:26:06,230
Two, how are we engaging with community members who are usually marginalized, usually not involved, and usually not engaged in community processes?

274
00:26:06,650 --> 00:26:15,353
So we're talking our Black BIPOC community, English-speaking Black BIPOC community, our Haitian Creole-speaking community,

275
00:26:17,253 --> 00:26:22,336
our Spanish-speaking community, our Portuguese-Brazilian community, and our Arabic-speaking community.

276
00:26:22,777 --> 00:26:28,761
So with that grant, over the last year and a half, we hired five liaisons and a coordinator, and we have done

277
00:26:29,863 --> 00:26:30,744
a great deal of work.

278
00:26:30,784 --> 00:26:34,526
I'm going to talk about the task, the community liaison in just one minute.

279
00:26:34,867 --> 00:26:46,855
And then the final bucket really is working with our organizations and coalescing around how we need to do a better job, reaching out, communicating, and being trusted and there for our community.

280
00:26:48,796 --> 00:26:58,779
We have just written another round of that funding, and that round of funding would then start to build resiliency hubs and a resiliency network is what we're calling it.

281
00:26:59,159 --> 00:27:03,000
So a physical location like community center where people could walk to walk in.

282
00:27:03,340 --> 00:27:04,140
That's the goal.

283
00:27:04,180 --> 00:27:04,840
That's our vision.

284
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:11,982
That's where we want to go is that we have multiple locations throughout the city that are trusted that people can go in and get help from and or

285
00:27:12,742 --> 00:27:23,090
have an enjoyable time in this community, do social activities, have a place that they can rely on that creates a sense of belonging in the community.

286
00:27:24,782 --> 00:27:25,702
But we're not there yet.

287
00:27:25,823 --> 00:27:35,527
So we're supporting multiple departments to start engaging in and agencies to start gauging into this process of actually getting to that physical location concept.

288
00:27:36,608 --> 00:27:45,472
And we're going to spend a lot of time if we get funded really trying to promote how to engage in health if you need it, as well as

289
00:27:47,233 --> 00:28:01,065
really translating and interpreting all the work that's being done in this community so that people can understand and communicate with us about what they want to see, what they need, and how to develop some leadership within their communities as well.

290
00:28:01,746 --> 00:28:10,955
But our five liaisons have been working really hard, and our Haitian liaison has created a task force.

291
00:28:10,975 --> 00:28:18,682
Our Haitian and our Arabic liaisons, they created task forces, people who are meeting regularly, once a month at least, if not twice a month.

292
00:28:19,682 --> 00:28:29,604
to talk about how they want to engage with the community, how they want the city to be more effective in working with the Haitian and the Arabic speaking communities.

293
00:28:30,144 --> 00:28:40,686
And we had on Saturday an event that was coordinated with the Medford Family Network and our Community Liaison Task Force for the Haitian Task Force.

294
00:28:41,086 --> 00:28:43,907
We had 75 adults come and 40 children.

295
00:28:45,127 --> 00:28:48,570
It was one of the best events I've ever been to in the city of Medford.

296
00:28:48,690 --> 00:28:49,671
It was exciting.

297
00:28:49,951 --> 00:28:50,812
It was fun.

298
00:28:50,972 --> 00:28:51,893
It was communal.

299
00:28:52,053 --> 00:28:56,256
It was, it was as though we were all just there to be together.

300
00:28:56,356 --> 00:29:03,622
You know, it wasn't so formal that we couldn't be human, but it wasn't so informal that it didn't feel like it had a purpose.

301
00:29:03,702 --> 00:29:05,344
And one of the big purpose was

302
00:29:05,824 --> 00:29:10,848
that they had a great speaker, Henry Malorin, who is a active member of the Medford community.

303
00:29:11,208 --> 00:29:13,910
And he really spoke to the Haitian community saying, you need to get involved.

304
00:29:13,950 --> 00:29:18,474
Let's start some leadership and let's get more involved in seeing how we want to see the city.

305
00:29:19,987 --> 00:29:21,648
be, we want to be a part of the leadership.

306
00:29:21,668 --> 00:29:26,931
So it was exactly what we were hoping for when we wrote this on a piece of paper a year and a half ago.

307
00:29:27,611 --> 00:29:36,757
And I just really hope that we can keep moving this along and continue to do this work because this is, this is the kind of stuff that will make a difference.

308
00:29:36,817 --> 00:29:40,859
And especially, you know, we're talking, you just talked about climate change, right?

309
00:29:40,899 --> 00:29:42,260
You have two heat waves in May.

310
00:29:42,920 --> 00:29:44,502
This is exactly what we're talking about.

311
00:29:44,542 --> 00:29:57,913
This is the future of our area, is unpredictable weather conditions, possible floods, possible need for heating or cooling centers, warming centers.

312
00:29:58,273 --> 00:30:04,218
So we need people in our community to know where to go so that they aren't suffering in their spaces without support.

313
00:30:04,338 --> 00:30:06,901
So that is all that we're doing.

314
00:30:07,041 --> 00:30:09,563
I forgot about that big, big project for a second.

315
00:30:10,013 --> 00:30:16,857
Hopefully we get funded, and we hope that we'll have more support through ARPA funding to keep doing this work.

316
00:30:18,758 --> 00:30:19,378
Thank you, Penny.

317
00:30:19,558 --> 00:30:25,161
Yeah, and the MVP grant has been really, really amazing, really helpful and beneficial.

318
00:30:25,721 --> 00:30:34,546
And one of the things that we did also as part of that grant, Penny, was we did an after-action review of COVID-19.

319
00:30:36,766 --> 00:30:47,207
And our internal communications and programming that we as a city did we held a couple of workshops with first with the health department, which was really.

320
00:30:48,943 --> 00:30:54,225
really helpful, kind of brought it all back and it was kind of cathartic.

321
00:30:55,806 --> 00:31:14,555
And then we did it with the department heads throughout the city as well, and other employees had a workshop with the mayor involved and really talked through, you know, it was really focused on those first three months of the pandemic and how we responded and how we communicated and, you know, what we did well, but certainly what we could have done better.

322
00:31:16,993 --> 00:31:30,058
I think that was really helpful and that's gonna help us moving forward again with planning for emergencies and preparedness for other climate emergencies or other things that come up.

323
00:31:30,398 --> 00:31:36,200
So we'll be focusing a lot on the findings of that after action review.

324
00:31:36,260 --> 00:31:40,721
